<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Spec section 20: no GPL dependency in the default build, ever (LIC-1). Every
    entry added here carries a licence note. Where GPL functionality is genuinely
    wanted it is invoked out of process as a tool the user supplies (RND-9,
    RND-15), never referenced from this file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The official MCP C# SDK, which section 20's dependency table names and
      marks "verify current version and licence". Verified: 2.2.0 declares
      Apache-2.0 as an SPDX expression in its own nuspec, and its whole
      transitive closure is ten Microsoft.Extensions.* packages, all MIT.

      The .Core package rather than the full one: it carries the server, the
      tool primitives and the stdio transport, and leaves behind the
      Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting integration this does not use.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="ModelContextProtocol.Core" Version="2.2.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The 3-D viewport. Section 16 names Helix Toolkit on its DirectX 11 path and says
      why: plain WPF Media3D cannot render 10^4 trajectories interactively.

      Verified rather than assumed, per section 20's "verify current version and
      licence" and r06's own caveat that third-party status must be re-checked before
      being committed to. 3.1.2 is MIT, read from the LICENSE file embedded in the
      package rather than from a URL, and its transitive closure is MIT throughout -
      CommunityToolkit, Cyotek, Microsoft.Extensions, and SharpDX. LIC-1 clear.

      TWO THINGS THE RE-CHECK FOUND, both recorded as an amendment in SPEC.md.

      The version contemporaneous with r06 no longer works: 2.27.3 is .NET Framework
      4.x only and restores on net10 solely through the NU1701 compatibility shim.
      3.1.2 restores cleanly, and is why this is pinned to the 3.x line.

      And every Helix DirectX package depends on SharpDX, ARCHIVED SINCE DECEMBER 2020.
      Taken knowingly. The blast radius is small by design: section 17 makes the
      interactive viewport "screen tuning, not an artifact", so publication figures come
      out of Einzel.Render - vector, headless, no third-party dependency. If SharpDX
      breaks on some future Windows we lose interactive tumbling and lose no figure, no
      number and no test. Vortice.Windows is the maintained successor and the exit if it
      ever bites; Helix has no Vortice backend today.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="HelixToolkit.Wpf.SharpDX" Version="3.1.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The cross-platform shell. Section 16's viewport has to run on Linux as well as
      Windows: the WPF shell is pinned to Windows by WPF itself, and its 3-D path rides
      on SharpDX, which the note above records as archived since December 2020. Avalonia
      is the replacement presentation layer, and UI-1 plus Amendment 25 are what make
      that a swap rather than a rewrite - the shell owns layout, input and the viewport
      and reaches the engine only through Einzel.Commands.

      Avalonia is 2-D, so the viewport is an OpenGL control and the drawing is ours.
      Silk.NET.OpenGL wraps the function pointers Avalonia's GlInterface hands over; it
      is a binding rather than an engine, and nothing below the shell sees it.

      Licenses verified rather than assumed, per section 20 and r06's own caveat. Both
      are MIT: Avalonia declares MIT in its nuspec and Silk.NET is MIT under the .NET
      Foundation. LIC-1 clear. Versions are the ones the spike proved on both platforms
      rather than the newest available, and the spike is the evidence that this path
      renders the engine's own exported conductor meshes rather than a typed triangle.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia" Version="11.2.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="11.2.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="11.2.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Silk.NET.OpenGL" Version="2.21.0" />

    <!--
      Transitively pinned, not chosen. Avalonia 11.2.3 brings Tmds.DBus.Protocol 0.20.0
      for the Linux session bus, and that version carries a known high-severity advisory
      (GHSA-xrw6-gwf8-vvr9). The repository builds with warnings as errors and NuGet
      auditing on, so it failed the build rather than passing as a note - which is the
      guard working. Central transitive pinning is enabled here, so the fixed version is
      named once and every project gets it. MIT, as the package it comes from is.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Tmds.DBus.Protocol" Version="0.95.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Test stack. MIT / Apache-2.0. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
